<section xml:id="notes-02-03">
  <title>Tuesday, Feb 3</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This is an outline of the topics we covered.
      You should not rely only on my notes.
      Make sure to take your own notes, and, if you miss a class, get notes from another student in class.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Mechanical Vibrations</title>

    <!-- TODO figure -->
    <p>
      Consider a mass attached to a spring, displaced from its resting position by <m>x(t)</m> at time <m>t</m>.
      The force acting on the mass is modeled by the differential equation:
      <md>
        <mrow> mx'' \amp = -c x' - kx +F(t). </mrow>
      </md>
      The constant <m>m</m> is the mass of the object.
      The term <m>-cx'</m> represents the contribution of friction, with a nonnegative constant <m>c</m>.
      The term <m>-kx</m>, with positive constant <m>k</m>, comes from Hooke's Law.
      The term <m>F(t)</m> represents an external force that may be applied.
      The system is called <term>forced</term> if <m>F(t)</m> is not identically zero.
      Otherwise, the system is called <term>unforced</term>.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Free undapmed motion</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>F(t) = 0, c = 0</m>.
          Then:
          <md>
            <mrow> mx'' + kx \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> x'' + \frac{k}{m} x \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> x'' + \omega_0^2 x \amp = 0, \quad \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{k}{m}} </mrow>
          </md>
          The roots of the characteristic polynomial are <m>\pm i \omega_0</m>, so the general solution is:
          <md>
            <mrow> x(t) \amp = A \cos(\omega_0 t) + B\sin(\omega_0 t) </mrow>
          </md>
          After applying trig identities, this can be rewritten:
          <md>
            <mrow> x(t) \amp = C \so(\omega_0 t - \gamma), </mrow>
          </md>
          where <m>C</m> is the <term>amplitude</term> of the wave, <m>\omega_0</m> is the <term>angular frequency</term>, and <m>\gamma</m> is the <term>phase shift</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Free damped motion</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider
          <md>
            <mrow> mx'' + cx' + kx \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> x'' + \frac{c}{m}x' + \frac{k}{m}x \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> x'' + 2p x' + \omega_0^2 x \amp = 0, \quad \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}, p = \frac{c}{2m} </mrow>
          </md>
          Then the roots of the characteristic equation are:
          <md>
            <mrow> r \amp = \frac{-2p \pm \sqrt{4p^2 - 4\omega_0^2}}{2} </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = -p \pm \sqrt{p^2 - \omega_0^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = -p \pm \sqrt{\frac{c^2}{4m^2} - \frac{k}{m}} </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = -p \pm \sqrt{\frac{c^2 - 4km}{4m^2}} </mrow>
          </md>
          We consider 3 cases.
        </p>

        <p>
          Case 1: if <m>c^2- 4km \gt 0</m>, the system is <term>overdamped</term>.
          The differential equation has 2 real roots <m>r_1, r_2 \lt 0</m>, so the general solution is:
          <md>
            <mrow> x(t) = C_1e^{r_1t} + C_2e^{r_2t} </mrow>
          </md>
          The solutions have no oscillation behavior, and <m>x(t) \to 0</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Case 2: if <m>c^2- 4km = 0</m>, the system is <term>critically damped</term>.
          The differential equation has a repeated real root <m>-p</m>, so the general solution is:
          <md>
            <mrow> x(t) = C_1e^{-pt} + C_2te^{-pt} </mrow>
          </md>
          Again, the solutions have no oscillation behavior, and <m>x(t) \to 0</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Case 3: if <m>c^2- 4km \lt 0</m>, the system is <term>underdamped</term>.
          The differential equation has complex conjugate roots <m>-p \pm i\omega_1</m>, where <m>\omega_1 = \sqrt{\omega_0^2 - p^2}</m> so the general solution is:
          <md>
            <mrow> x(t) = Ae^{-pt} \cos(\omega_1 t) + B e^{-pt} \sin(\omega_1 t) </mrow>
          </md>
          The solutions show oscillation behavior, and <m>x(t) \to 0</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      What if <m>F(t)</m> is not identically zero? Then the differential equation
      <md>
        <mrow> mx'' + cx' + kx = F(t) </mrow>
      </md>
      is not homogeneous.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>